# Current cost review — frozen HELD publication diagnostic

Read-only review, 22 October 2026. No runtime candidate, source edit, gate, build, or timing was produced by this review. The existing dirty experiment files and archived controls were left untouched. Parity across the fixed seven families remains unmet.

## Evidence and scope

The one existing diagnostic pair used `benchmark:edit-sized-publication-profile` with `bulk-write`, `publication`, 1,024 owners, first position, 10 warmups, 80 measured callbacks, no timed reads, and the scene publication path. Each complete callback included 1,024 synchronous text setters followed by one publication. The frozen HELD archive was SHA-256 `06fe22b7f05dceb27f59b2e82d33fab541c8cee04c37b1a1ccdf078f6ecdc9be`; the frozen main archive was SHA-256 `bbb3375c08fb3db8186ec10e4b766b7170632f7601eb1ec0e90be0197f22eb20`. Both named Wasm profiles were authenticated to their respective shipping Wasm. See `profile-pair/paired-report.md`, `profile-pair/{held-06fe,main-4e25}/{manifest.json,postflight.json,cpu-profile.json}`, `profile-pair/setter-raw-frames.md`, and `profile-pair/callback-analysis.json`.

The HELD complete-callback nearest-rank p50/p90/p95 was 13.9525/14.9594/15.2425 ms (n=80). The main arm was unstable: 20.4961/181.8966/280.2592 ms, with 77 profiler time deltas above 5 ms. The pair cannot rank candidate mechanisms causally. The corrected, separately run fixed20 acceptance comparison still has a HELD 1,024 publication p50 gap of 2.292 ms to main and 2.328 ms to published 0.1.0; its 512 rewrite gap to main is 1.361 ms. Those fixed20 operations and this diagnostic callback have different timing protocols, so their values are not algebraic parts of one budget.

## Disjoint named self samples under the HELD setter anchor

The raw setter anchor is node 7. I independently aggregated raw V8 `timeDeltas` for nodes whose parent chain includes that anchor, preserving exclusive self attribution. These groups use distinct raw node/function sets; they do not double-count inclusive descendants. Values divided by 80 are descriptive sampled CPU per callback, not elapsed phase time or removable savings.

| Named group | Exclusive self µs across 80 callbacks | Descriptive ms/callback | Interpretation |
| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |
| `frame-wire.js` JavaScript, six nodes | 39,668 | 0.496 | Generic request preparation/writing functions; even a compact descriptor cannot assume every sample disappears. |
| Rust `parse_update_request` and `parse_text_mutations`, two nodes | 4,207 | 0.053 | Named parser leaves; inlined parse work may appear in parents. |
| Rust `pmndrs_glyph_engine_measure_paragraph` entry and wrapper names, four nodes | 16,127 | 0.202 | Mixed dispatch and entry work; the same Rust executor remains necessary. |
| Rust `validate_planner_inputs` and `validate_text_mutations`, three nodes | 29,501 | 0.369 | Input/preparation validation; most obligations remain with a new descriptor. |
| Rust `commit_all` and `commit_paragraphs`, two nodes | 13,833 | 0.173 | Candidate adoption/commit work; the retained state must still commit. |

The whole setter anchor has 534 samples and 755,125 µs of exclusive self weight. Its origin buckets are JavaScript 146,081 µs, allocation 4,208 µs, host runtime 1,291 µs, shaping 226,211 µs, and other Wasm 377,334 µs. **JavaScript plus allocation plus host runtime (151,580 µs) is not an upper envelope for compact-descriptor work:** `other Wasm` includes parser, entry, validation, and commit names, as well as semantic algorithms. Conversely, it is not a removable-cost total. Raw self samples in named parent frames can include inlined or unresolved child instructions; the minified JavaScript and `binaryen:byn$...` Wasm nodes also limit attribution. Detached samples remain unresolved. Do not add inclusive frame totals, sum marginal medians, or turn these sampled weights into a hard latency bound.

## Architecture judgment

A private full-replacement descriptor could bypass some generic frame writing and parsing while feeding the same Rust executor. The actual current path in `packages/glyph/src/internal/render-planner.ts` (`#prepareTextCandidate`) constructs a single text mutation and synchronously calls `prepareParagraph`; `packages/glyph/src/internal/handle-state.ts` writes the request and reads the result; `packages/glyph/rust/shaper/src/wasm.rs` parses it, calls `measure_paragraph_with_shaper`, stages the query result, and commits with `commit_measure`. The descriptor would still need caller-value bounds, revision/generation fencing, preflight, shaping, flow, positioning, status/failure attribution, Rust commit, and TypeScript adoption. It would change the raw Wasm ABI and require corresponding artifact, correctness, rollback, and downstream checks. The directly named writer/parser self samples are small relative to the fixed20 1,024 p50 gap, while entry and other parent/inlined costs cannot be priced from this profile. Therefore this review does not admit an ABI change or a runtime candidate. It does not prove such a change can never help.

The cluster algorithm is a genuine, larger but unpriced trade-off. In `packages/glyph/rust/shaper/src/engine/state.rs`, `prepare_clusters` deliberately skips `rebuild_source_run_if_topology_is_stable` for the full single-source-run case with matching grapheme/text cardinality, selecting `ClusterArena::build`. The full builder in `cluster_state.rs` clears/reserves and pushes current SoA cluster fields, rebuilds the index, aggregates shape, rebuilds layout runs, and refreshes units. The stable builder first copies roughly two dozen prior SoA lanes and layout-run records, then overwrites each affected cluster and glyph; main's source admits that path, and its profile includes a `copy_from` frame under publication. Removing old-row copies reduced counted work, but does not establish lower complete-operation cost: full construction also pays pushes/reserves and downstream scans. The HELD `prepare_clusters` root subtrees total 133,666 µs of inclusive sampled weight across this capture, about 1.67 ms per callback descriptively, including essential cluster work. The main arm's unstable tail rules out comparing its cluster sample weights as a causal ranking. Neither path has a credible measured saving toward the 2.292/2.328 ms 1,024 fixed20 gap, and a 1,024-only result would not settle the other six families.

If a later decision calls for one discriminator, an isolated current-architecture comparison that changes only full-source-run admission to the existing stable builder, proves identical output/path counts, and measures complete 512/1,024 operations against frozen HELD would test this specific trade-off. This is a conditional option, not a selected experiment or authorization to run one now. No broad profile or ABI change is justified by the current evidence alone.
